/E (ABSTRACT)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Last-mile delivery is the final and operationally complex stage of a logistics chain. Orders arrive over time while road congestion, deadlines, partner availability, distance, and workload change continuously. SmartRoute AI is proposed as an explainable and risk-aware Deep Reinforcement Learning \(DRL\) framework for dynamic assignment of delivery orders to available partners. Its state representation combines order attributes, partner status, estimated travel time, distance, traffic conditions, and delivery priority.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (A DRL agent learns an assignment policy through interaction with a software simulation environment. The reward design penalises delay, distance, operational risk, and workload imbalance while rewarding on-time delivery. The Hungarian Algorithm is retained as a classical baseline, and a nearest-partner rule provides a simple comparison. An explainability layer generates a concise reason for each assignment using travel time, risk, and workload.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (The planned evaluation includes mean delivery time, on-time rate, assignment agreement with the Hungarian baseline on static snapshots, total distance, workload distribution, risk score, feasibility, and explanation coverage. Since the source paper identifies the project as being at the design stage, this report describes the proposed architecture and evaluation plan rather than claiming measured improvements.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Keywords: last-mile delivery, dynamic assignment, deep reinforcement learning, explainable )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
