389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E (Last-mile delivery refers to the movement of goods from a local distribution point to the final customer. Although it is the final stage of a shipment, it can be difficult to coordinate because many small deliveries must be completed across a road network with variable traffic and different deadlines. A dispatcher must select a suitable partner while considering current location, workload, availability, distance, and the likelihood of meeting the promised delivery time.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 206 0 R
/T (1.2 Problem Statement)
/E (1.2 Problem Statement)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E (Delivery assignment must balance delivery time, travel distance, partner availability, deadlines, congestion, and workload. Static optimisation can produce a good assignment for a fixed snapshot, but its result may lose quality when conditions change. Simple rules are fast but may ignore future workload and combined risk. A learned model can adapt to experience, yet a black-box decision is difficult to audit without a useful explanation. The problem is to design a dynamic assignment framework that considers risk, compares with established baselines, and explains each decision.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 206 0 R
/T (1.3 Objectives)
/E (1.3 Objectives)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E (The project objectives are to train a DRL policy for dynamic assignment; represent order, partner, traffic, and deadline information; include congestion, delay risk, excess distance, and workload in a risk score; design a reward that balances service quality and operational cost; compare against the Hungarian Algorithm and nearest-partner rule; and produce human-readable explanations.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 206 0 R
/T (1.4 Scope and Expected Outcomes)
/E (1.4 Scope and Expected Outcomes)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E (The scope covers a simulator, state representation, risk-aware reward calculation, candidate DRL policy, baseline methods, explanation generation, and repeatable evaluation. It does not yet constitute a complete production routing platform. Expected outcomes include a reproducible software framework and evidence about trade-offs under light, moderate, and heavy demand. A learned policy should not be assumed to outperform every baseline on every metric.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
