/P 207 0 R
/T (1.6 Operational Context of Dynamic Dispatch)
/E (1.6 Operational Context of Dynamic Dispatch)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
405 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 207 0 R
/E (Delivery platforms receive requests continuously rather than in a single batch. Each request carries a pickup or drop location, a promised delivery window, and in many cases a priority level such as standard, express, or time-critical. At any moment a dispatcher must decide which of the currently available partners should accept an order, and this decision must be made quickly because customers and merchants expect prompt confirmation. The quality of a decision depends on information that changes between one decision and the next: the position of every partner, the number of orders each partner is already carrying, and the traffic conditions on candidate routes.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 207 0 R
/E (Because decisions are taken one after another, an early choice influences the options that remain. Assigning a nearby partner to an order may remove that partner from consideration for a more urgent request that arrives a minute later. Likewise, repeatedly assigning work to the same partner may produce fatigue, late deliveries, or unfair earnings. These effects are not visible in a one-shot cost matrix, which is the main reason this project formulates assignment as a sequential decision problem and not as a collection of independent calculations.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 207 0 R
/E (Operational teams also require accountability. When a delivery is late, a supervisor may need to know why a particular partner was chosen and whether a better alternative existed. A system that cannot give a short and truthful reason for its decisions is difficult to adopt even if its aggregate performance is good. SmartRoute AI therefore treats explanation as part of the decision pipeline and not as an afterthought.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
