442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 209 0 R
/E (Dantzig and Ramser introduced the truck dispatching problem, which became a foundation for vehicle-routing research [15]. Boysen, Fedtke, and Schwerdfeger surveyed last-mile delivery concepts and described the operational pressure created by customer time expectations and diverse delivery models [1]. These works establish the importance of efficient planning, but they do not by themselves provide an adaptive policy for every newly arriving order.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 209 0 R
/E (A delivery decision depends on the state at the moment of assignment. A nearby partner may already carry several orders, while a more distant partner may have a lower workload or a more reliable path under current congestion. Distance and time matter, but so do deadlines, availability, risk, and the effect of the decision on future orders.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
