<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 210 0 R
/T (2.6 Assignment as a Markov Decision Process)
/E (2.6 Assignment as a Markov Decision Process)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 210 0 R
/E (A Markov Decision Process \(MDP\) is defined by a set of states, a set of actions, a transition function, a reward function, and a discount factor. In the delivery setting, a state contains the pending orders, the partner positions and loads, and the current traffic profile. An action assigns a pending order to one eligible partner. The transition function describes how the simulator advances time, updates workload, introduces new orders, and changes travel times. The reward function converts the immediate consequences of an assignment into a scalar signal. Sutton and Barto describe this framework and the associated notions of return and value functions in detail [16].)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 210 0 R
/E (The discount factor controls how strongly the agent values future consequences. A value close to one encourages the agent to consider the effect of an assignment on later orders, which is important when partner availability is limited. A small value makes the policy short-sighted and closer to a greedy rule. The choice of discount factor is therefore a design parameter that should be examined during validation instead of being fixed arbitrarily.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 210 0 R
/E (The Markov property requires the state to contain all information needed to predict the future. If the state omits partner workload or traffic level, the agent cannot learn their influence and the problem becomes partially observable. This observation motivates the inclusion of workload, deadline, and traffic features in the state vector and supports the requirement that the state builder be tested independently.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 210 0 R
/T (2.7 Value-Based and Policy-Gradient Learning)
/E (2.7 Value-Based and Policy-Gradient Learning)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 210 0 R
/E (Deep Q-Networks approximate the action-value function with a neural network and learn from stored transitions using a replay buffer and a periodically updated target network [3]. They are well suited to discrete action spaces, such as choosing one partner from a list. Their main weaknesses are sensitivity to hyperparameters, overestimation of action values, and difficulty when the number of actions changes from step to step.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 210 0 R
/E (Proximal Policy Optimisation belongs to the policy-gradient family. It optimises a clipped surrogate objective that limits the size of each policy update, which usually improves training stability [4]. PPO can represent a stochastic policy and can be combined with action masking so that infeasible partners receive zero probability. It typically needs more environment interaction than a value-based method but is often easier to tune.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 210 0 R
/E (For SmartRoute AI both algorithms are retained as candidates. The final selection should be based on observed stability across seeds, compatibility with variable numbers of eligible partners, )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
