>>
endobj
465 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 211 0 R
/T (2.8 Dynamic Routing and Dispatch Considerations)
/E (2.8 Dynamic Routing and Dispatch Considerations)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
466 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (Research on the vehicle-routing problem began with the truck dispatching formulation of Dantzig and Ramser [15] and has since expanded to include time windows, heterogeneous fleets, and stochastic demand. Learning-based approaches such as the pointer-network method of Nazari et al. [5] and the attention model of Kool et al. [6] construct routes sequentially and can generalise across instances drawn from a distribution. Large-scale dispatch systems, such as those studied by Xu et al. [7] and Lin et al. [8], combine learned value estimates with planning or coordination to manage thousands of vehicles.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
467 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (These studies are valuable precedents, but they differ from the present problem in important ways. Ride-hailing dispatch typically optimises passenger waiting time and driver idle time, whereas last-mile delivery adds parcel deadlines, multi-order workloads, and priority classes. Routing methods optimise the sequence of stops for a given vehicle, while SmartRoute AI concentrates on the earlier question of which partner should receive each order. The two problems are complementary, and joint treatment is identified as future work.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
468 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 211 0 R
/T (2.9 Interpretability in Operational Decision Support)
/E (2.9 Interpretability in Operational Decision Support)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
469 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (An explanation is useful only if it is faithful to the decision and understandable to the person reading it. Surveys of explainable reinforcement learning distinguish between methods that explain individual decisions, methods that summarise policy behaviour, and methods that make the learning process itself transparent [11], [12]. Feature-attribution techniques such as SHAP [9] and LIME [10] fit the first category, and they have been widely applied to supervised models.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
470 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (Applying such techniques to sequential decision making raises additional issues. The same feature may matter differently at different times, and an attribution computed around one state may not describe the policy elsewhere. For this reason the proposed explanation layer begins with direct comparison of travel time, risk, and workload between the selected partner and the alternatives, which is simple and easy to audit. Attribution methods are considered as optional extensions whose stability must be checked.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
471 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 211 0 R
/T (2.10 Comparative Discussion of Related Approaches)
/E (2.10 Comparative Discussion of Related Approaches)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
472 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (Classical assignment methods guarantee optimality for a fixed cost matrix but do not model the future. Heuristic rules are fast and transparent but ignore interactions between decisions. Learning-based dispatch can capture temporal effects, yet it requires a simulator, careful reward engineering, and a way to build trust in its choices. Safe reinforcement learning introduces constraints or risk measures [13], [14], but is frequently studied in control tasks instead of delivery operations.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (No single approach covers every requirement. The proposed framework keeps the optimisation baseline as a reference, uses a learned policy to handle sequential effects, expresses risk as part of the reward and the state, and generates explanations for each decision. The comparison in later chapters is intended to reveal where each method is strong and where it is weak, not to assume the superiority of the learned policy.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
