548 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Several design decisions involve trade-offs that are recorded here for transparency. First, a single scalar reward is easy to optimise but hides the relative importance of its components; the framework therefore also logs the components separately. Second, a rich state improves the information available to the agent but enlarges the network and the training cost; scaling and a bounded candidate list limit this growth. Third, a simulator is repeatable and safe, but it cannot reproduce all real-world behaviour; results must therefore be described as simulation findings.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
549 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (Finally, the explanation layer favours direct feature comparison over complex attribution because simple explanations are easier to verify. This choice may omit subtle interactions, and the trade-off is accepted for the initial version.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
