615 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 221 0 R
/E (Three demand levels are planned. In the light setting the number of partners exceeds the number of simultaneously pending orders, so most orders have several feasible candidates. In the moderate setting the fleet is used at a substantial fraction of its capacity. In the heavy setting orders frequently compete for partners, deadlines are tight, and feasibility constraints are often binding. Each demand level can be combined with normal or congested traffic.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
616 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 221 0 R
/E (Spatial layouts may be uniform, clustered around a few demand centres, or mixed. Clustered layouts are useful for testing whether the policy balances workload when many orders originate from the same area. Deadline slack is drawn from a distribution whose lower tail produces occasional urgent orders, allowing the effect of priority handling to be examined.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
617 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 221 0 R
/T (4.9 Feasibility Masking and Action Selection)
/E (4.9 Feasibility Masking and Action Selection)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
618 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 221 0 R
/E (A pair of order and partner is feasible when the partner is available, has remaining capacity, and can reach the order within an acceptable travel time under the current traffic profile. The feasibility mask is a binary vector over the candidate actions. During training and evaluation, actions with a mask value of zero are removed from consideration so that the agent cannot select an invalid partner.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
619 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 221 0 R
/E (For value-based learning, the Q-values of masked actions are replaced by a very low number before the maximum is taken. For policy-gradient learning, the logits of masked actions are set to a very low value before the softmax, which yields zero probability for them. If every candidate is masked, the environment records a deferral event and keeps the order in the queue, optionally with a penalty that reflects the waiting cost.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
620 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 221 0 R
/E (Using a mask rather than penalising invalid actions after the fact shortens training and removes a source of unintended behaviour, because the agent does not need to learn constraints that the simulator can enforce directly.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
621 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 221 0 R
/T (4.10 Training Procedure)
/E (4.10 Training Procedure)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
622 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 221 0 R
/E (Training is carried out in episodes. At the start of an episode the environment is reset with a scenario configuration and a seed. The agent observes the state, selects a feasible action, and receives the reward and next state. Transitions are stored in a buffer for DQN or collected in rollouts for PPO. The network parameters are updated at regular intervals until the planned number of environment steps has been completed.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
