/T (4.11 Hungarian Baseline Construction)
/E (4.11 Hungarian Baseline Construction)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
632 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 222 0 R
/E (For the static baseline, the pending orders and the available partners at a decision time are arranged in a cost matrix. Each entry combines the estimated travel time and the risk score for the corresponding pair, using the same weights as the reward where appropriate. Infeasible pairs receive a very large cost so that they are not chosen when a feasible alternative exists. If there are more orders than partners, or the reverse, the matrix is padded with dummy rows or columns to make it square [2].)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
633 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 222 0 R
/E (The Hungarian Algorithm then returns the assignment with the minimum total cost for this matrix. Because new orders arrive later, the algorithm is invoked again at each decision time with the updated state. This rolling use is a reasonable baseline for dynamic operation but is not equivalent to solving the entire future problem, and the report should describe it as such.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
634 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 222 0 R
/T (4.12 Nearest-Partner Baseline Procedure)
/E (4.12 Nearest-Partner Baseline Procedure)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 222 0 R
/E (The nearest-partner rule examines the feasible partners for an order and selects the one with the smallest distance or estimated travel time. Ties are broken by a fixed rule, for example by the lower current workload and then by identifier, so that results are repeatable. The rule requires no training and negligible computation, which makes it a useful lower bound on the complexity that a learned method must justify.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
