>>
endobj
665 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 223 0 R
/E (A small numerical example illustrates how the components of the framework interact. Suppose a new order arrives with a moderate deadline and three partners are eligible. Partner A is closest but already carries two active orders. Partner B is slightly farther and carries one order. Partner C is the farthest and has no active order. The state builder scales the distances, estimated travel times, and workloads and passes them to the policy together with the feasibility mask.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
666 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 223 0 R
/E (The risk module computes a score for each pair. Because partner A has the highest workload and its route crosses a congested zone, its risk score is highest. Partner C has the lowest risk but the longest travel time. Partner B lies between the two on both measures. The nearest-partner rule would select A because of distance alone. The Hungarian Algorithm would select the pair with the lowest combined cost for the supplied matrix, which may be B or C depending on the weights.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
