<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 226 0 R
/T (5.4 Expected Analysis and Limitations)
/E (5.4 Expected Analysis and Limitations)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
695 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 226 0 R
/E (The framework may offer a flexible way to study changing delivery conditions because the policy can learn from sequential feedback. The risk module makes operational risk explicit, workload balancing aims to reduce task concentration, and the explanation layer may improve auditability. These are design expectations, not demonstrated findings. A simulator approximates real traffic, order patterns, and partner behaviour; strong simulated performance may not transfer directly to a city deployment. Reward weights influence learned behaviour, DRL does not guarantee global optimality, and risk scores depend on input quality and assumptions. Explanations may be misleading if they do not faithfully reflect the decision process. Future evaluation should include sensitivity analysis, stress tests, explanation audits, and eventually a limited pilot using real order and traffic data with appropriate privacy controls and a fallback dispatch procedure.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
696 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 226 0 R
