/T (5.8 Evaluation of Explanations)
/E (5.8 Evaluation of Explanations)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
718 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Explanation quality is evaluated separately from decision quality. Coverage measures the proportion of assignments that have an explanation. Fidelity measures whether the factors mentioned in the explanation correspond to the logged values; this can be checked automatically by recomputing the comparison. Consistency measures whether similar situations receive similar explanations.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
719 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Human review can complement the automatic checks. A small group of reviewers may read a sample of explanations and judge whether they are clear and whether they would help in accepting or rejecting the recommendation. Because such a study involves people, it should be described as a future activity and conducted with appropriate consent.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
720 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 227 0 R
/T (5.9 Threats to Validity)
/E (5.9 Threats to Validity)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
721 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Internal validity may be reduced by implementation errors, unequal treatment of methods, or tuning on the test scenarios. These risks are mitigated by shared interfaces, paired scenarios, separation of training, validation, and test seeds, and early validation of the environment with simple baselines.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
722 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (External validity concerns how far simulation results apply to real operations. The synthetic traffic and order models may not capture local road networks, driver behaviour, or demand patterns. Statements about real-world benefit are therefore outside the scope of the present study and would require validation against historical data and a controlled pilot.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Construct validity concerns whether the metrics measure what matters. A low mean delivery time may hide a few very late orders, and a high on-time rate may be achieved by rejecting difficult orders. Reporting several complementary metrics, including percentiles of delivery time and the number of deferrals, reduces this risk.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 227 0 R
/T (5.10 Acceptance Criteria for the Prototype)
/E (5.10 Acceptance Criteria for the Prototype)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 227 0 R
/E (Before the framework is described as complete, the following criteria should be satisfied. The simulator reproduces the same scenario from the same seed. All planned tests in Section 5.3 have been run and their status updated. Each method produces only feasible assignments or defined deferrals. Every assignment has an explanation that passes the fidelity check. Results are reported for all scenario groups with the number of seeds and a measure of variability. Any limitation that affects interpretation is stated in the report.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
