738 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 229 0 R
/T (6.1 Conclusion)
/E (6.1 Conclusion)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
739 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 229 0 R
/T (SmartRoute AI is proposed as an explainable and risk-aware DRL framework for dynamic last-mile delivery assignment. It combines order requests, partner status, traffic estimates, distance, deadlines, and priority in a state representation. A DRL agent selects feasible assignments and receives reward feedback based on delay, distance, operational risk, and workload imbalance, with a bonus for on-time delivery. The Hungarian Algorithm provides a classical baseline and a nearest-partner rule offers a simple comparison point.)
/E (SmartRoute AI is proposed as an explainable and risk-aware DRL framework for dynamic last-mile delivery assignment. It combines order requests, partner status, traffic estimates, distance, deadlines, and priority in a state representation. A DRL agent selects feasible assignments and receives reward feedback based on delay, distance, operational risk, and workload imbalance, with a bonus for on-time delivery. The Hungarian Algorithm provides a classical baseline and a nearest-partner rule offers a simple comparison point.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
740 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 229 0 R
/T (The framework includes an explanation layer that reports why a partner was selected using travel-time estimates, risk, and workload. Its modular architecture is intended for evaluation in a software simulator using repeatable scenarios and common metrics. Its contribution is the integration of dynamic learning, explicit risk awareness, workload balance, baseline comparison, and per-decision explanation in one proposed system. No numerical improvement is claimed because the supplied source material does not include completed simulation results.)
/E (The framework includes an explanation layer that reports why a partner was selected using travel-time estimates, risk, and workload. Its modular architecture is intended for evaluation in a software simulator using repeatable scenarios and common metrics. Its contribution is the integration of dynamic learning, explicit risk awareness, workload balance, baseline comparison, and per-decision explanation in one proposed system. No numerical improvement is claimed because the supplied source material does not include completed simulation results.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
741 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 229 0 R
/T (6.2 Future Enhancements)
/E (6.2 Future Enhancements)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
742 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 229 0 R
/T (Future work should complete the simulator, train candidate DQN and PPO agents, run controlled experiments across order intensity and traffic, perform sensitivity analysis on reward weights, and validate the simulator against historical delivery data. Further extensions may jointly optimise assignment and route planning, explore multi-agent coordination, integrate a dispatcher dashboard, audit explanation quality, test robustness to missing data and abrupt traffic changes, and conduct a limited real-world pilot with operational safeguards.)
/E (Future work should complete the simulator, train candidate DQN and PPO agents, run controlled experiments across order intensity and traffic, perform sensitivity analysis on reward weights, and validate the simulator against historical delivery data. Further extensions may jointly optimise assignment and route planning, explore multi-agent coordination, integrate a dispatcher dashboard, audit explanation quality, test robustness to missing data and abrupt traffic changes, and conduct a limited real-world pilot with operational safeguards.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
743 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 229 0 R
/T (6.3 Recommendations for Final Validation)
/E (6.3 Recommendations for Final Validation)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
744 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 229 0 R
/T (Before deployment, acceptance thresholds should be agreed for service quality, feasibility, explanation quality, runtime performance, and risk handling. A fallback method should be available when no feasible assignment exists or the learned policy is unsuitable. Model updates should be versioned and tested against a fixed benchmark suite. Any operational claim should be supported by measurements from the relevant environment.)
/E (Before deployment, acceptance thresholds should be agreed for service quality, feasibility, explanation quality, runtime performance, and risk handling. A fallback method should be available when no feasible assignment exists or the learned policy is unsuitable. Model updates should be versioned and tested against a fixed benchmark suite. Any operational claim should be supported by measurements from the relevant environment.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
