endobj
891 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 230 0 R
/T (6.4 Ethical and Social Considerations)
/E (6.4 Ethical and Social Considerations)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
892 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Automated assignment affects people. Partners depend on assignments for income, and a policy that systematically sends difficult or poorly paid orders to the same individuals would be unfair. The workload term in the reward is intended to reduce this concentration, but fairness should also be measured directly, for example by comparing the distribution of orders and distances across partners.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
893 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Transparency toward partners is also important. They should be able to understand the general rules used to allocate work and to raise concerns about particular decisions. The decision records and explanations proposed in this report can support such a process, provided that they are presented in an understandable form.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
894 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Finally, the system should be used as decision support. A human dispatcher should be able to override a recommendation, and a fallback rule should be available when the learned policy is unsuitable. These safeguards reduce the harm that could result from an unforeseen failure of the model.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
895 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 230 0 R
/T (6.5 Summary of Contributions)
/E (6.5 Summary of Contributions)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
896 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (This report has proposed an integrated framework for dynamic last-mile delivery assignment. Its contributions are the formulation of assignment as a sequential decision problem, a risk-aware reward with separately logged components, a feasibility-masked action space, a fair baseline comparison with the Hungarian Algorithm and a nearest-partner rule, a template-based explanation procedure grounded in measured feature differences, and an evaluation plan that emphasises reproducibility and honest reporting.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
897 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (The next step is to implement the simulator and agent as outlined, execute the planned tests, and report the measured outcomes with their limitations. Only after those results are available can the benefits of the framework be assessed.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
898 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 230 0 R
/T (6.6 Lessons for Practice)
/E (6.6 Lessons for Practice)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
899 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Several practical lessons follow from the design process. First, the simulator is as important as the learning algorithm; an inaccurate environment can mislead the agent and the reader. Second, simple baselines are valuable because they expose errors in the environment and provide a reference for judging complexity. Third, a multi-term reward must be monitored component by component, since the sum can conceal undesirable behaviour. Fourth, explanations should be generated from the same data as the decision and checked automatically.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
900 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 230 0 R
/E (Finally, honest reporting is part of engineering quality. A framework that is described accurately, with its assumptions and limitations, is more useful to a reader than one that claims unsupported gains. The structure of this report was chosen to keep that distinction visible.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
